  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 324 PM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES WV imagery places the UP just downstream of a mid \nlevel trough over northern MN pivoting toward it with a northward \nlifting ridge over the northern Atlantic coastline. The cold front \nand stream of steadier rain has pressed east outside the CWA, \nleaving trailing light rain showers, mainly over the northern tier \nas the pattern begins to change. Afternoon temps currently ranging \nthe mid 30s to mid 40s continue to fall in its wake. 850/925 mb RAP \nanalysis as well as sfc obs over the western spine of the UP, Lake \nSuperior and upstream into northern MN and northwest Ontario show a \nconvergent signal. This in tandem with the incoming trough aloft and \n850 mb temps falling to -13C to -15C for Monday support a transition \nover to LES with a strong dominant band expected to develop over the \nwestern UP. There is moderate confidence in exact placement of this \nband, but the area of current focus is between Ontonagon, \nPainesdale, Herman, Covington, and Bruce Crossing. \n\nWarm lake waters between 7-8C, a mixed marine layer and lake effect \nclouds/precip will limit temps tonight into Monday morning. \nMaintained similar edits to previous forecast packages, keeping lows \nin the mid 20s to low 30s (closer to raw model guidance vs the NBM). \nThe timing of falling air temps to and below freezing and ground \ntemps currently in the 40s will be a limiting factor with snow, \ninfluencing SLRs. Model soundings within this band signal noteworthy \nthermodynamics (inversion heights through the DGZ up to 10-15 kft \nalongside marginal lake induced CAPE) with sufficient/deep moisture \nthat should overpower the otherwise marginal profile elsewhere \nduring this event. Another wave dropping south-southeast over the UP \non Monday may yield a second period of low level convergence with \nsimilar thermodynamics and supportive sfc temps near or below \nfreezing in the north for higher impacts during the morning commute. \nThat said, confidence on this was lower than the first wave. \nGenerally expecting widespread SLRs below 10:1 until Monday when \ncolder air arrives, but higher SLRs in the mid to upper teens \naccompany the dominant banding expected. With a QPF footprint of 0.1 \nto 0.4 inches with higher amounts up to 0.75 in the western area of \nfocus, general LES amounts are expected between 2 and 5 inches over \nthe northwest wind LES belts by the end of Monday. Mainly 1 inch or \nless in the far eastern and south-central UP. An area of 6 to 8 \ninches is likely (45-70% chance) underneath the dominant band. Heavy \nsnowfall rates up to 1 inch/hr at times will quickly reduce \nvisibility below 1 mile (25-50% chance, highest in the dominant band \nover the west). Opted for Winter Weather Advisories Tonight through \nMonday morning (0Z-16Z Monday) for Ontonagon, Houghton and Baraga \nCounties; Gogebic starts a few hours later at 3Z. Note that Heaviest \nsnowfall is expected to remain south of Houghton. If confidence in \nthe Monday second wave increases, additional Winter Weather \nAdvisories and/or extensions may be warranted.\n\nRidging aloft and high pressure/drier air at the surface building in \nput an end to LES by Monday evening in the west and late Monday \nnight in the east. Temps fall much colder in the teens and 20s\nMonday night, coldest interior west. High pressure dominates \nthe remainder of the work week, likely keeping the UP dry. A \nnorthward lifting Atlantic low pressure system only grazes the \nUP with low PoPs (15-30%) Thursday into Friday. Otherwise \nseasonable temps with highs in the 30s to low 40s and lows in \nthe teens and 20s (save for low 30s at the shores) return.",
